| `leadingLink` | `{ href: string; label: string }` |  | Makes `leading` a link, such as an app's mark that goes home: `{ href: '/', label: 'Home' }`. The label names the link, since the picture has no text of its own. |
| `back` | `{ href: string; label: string } \| { label: string; onSelect: () => void }` |  | Back for nested screens: a link, such as `{ href: '/settings', label: 'Settings' }`, or a button that acts in place, such as `{ label: 'Inbox', onSelect: close }` for a detail that opened inside the page. A button moves no focus itself, so `onSelect` decides where focus returns, such as to the control that opened the detail. |
| `backOnEscape` | `boolean` | `true` | Escape activates the back link, unless a layer above it took the key first: an open menu or dialog, or the focused field when its text changed since it took focus. It doesn't see a form's other fields, so a form that must keep Escape while it has unsaved changes turns this off while dirty, as ProfileFlow does. Default `true` when `back` is set on a level 1 heading. A level 2 or 3 header sits inside another screen, so its Escape is off unless you turn it on. |
| `actions` | `React.ReactNode` |  | One or two Buttons, at most one of them filled. |
| `headingLevel` | `1 \| 2 \| 3` | `1` | Heading level: 1 for a screen's title, 2 for a header shown inside another page, or 3 for one under a heading of that page, such as a chat message's author. Level 3 draws a smaller title. Default 1. |
| `focusOnMount` | `boolean` | `true` | Focus the heading on mount, and again each time this turns `true`, so people using a keyboard or a screen reader land on the new screen. A screen that shows its list again after a detail, such as InboxFlow, sets it back to `true` to land there. It yields when something else in the same commit moved focus inside its `<main>`. Default `true`. |
| `headingRef` | `React.Ref<HTMLHeadingElement>` |  | The heading element, for moving focus here after an action removes the focused control. |
| `className` | `string` |  |  |

## Example

The smallest working use, from the docs site:

```tsx
import { PageHeader } from '@/components/lede/blocks/page-header';
import { Button } from '@/components/lede/button';

// The screen's one h1. It takes focus when the screen mounts.
export function InboxHeader({ unread, total }: { unread: number; total: number }) {
  return (
    <PageHeader
      title="Inbox"
      summary={`${unread} unread of ${total}`}
      back={{ href: '/', label: 'Today' }}
      actions={<Button size="sm">Select</Button>}
    />
  );
}
```

Docs: [Blocks](https://lede.cloudcompute.com/blocks.md).

